<Wiki
  title="puv remove"
  description="Drop a dependency from the manifest, write a new lock, and sync the project."
>
  <p class="kicker">Wiki</p>
  <h1>remove</h1>
  <p>
    <code>puv remove</code> drops a package, relocks, and syncs. It is the reverse of
    <a href="/wiki/commands/add"><code>puv add</code></a>.
  </p>

  <h2>A runtime dependency</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv remove symfony/console</pre>
  <p>
    The line leaves <code>[dependencies]</code>. puv resolves again, writes <code>puv.lock</code>,
    and installs that lock. Packages that were only present because <code>symfony/console</code>
    required them leave the graph when nothing else requires them.
  </p>

  <h2>A dev dependency</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv remove phpunit/phpunit</pre>
  <p>
    The same command removes a package that was added with <code>--dev</code>. The argument is
    the package name.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does not remove</h2>
  <p>
    The bytes stay in <code>~/.cache/puv/</code> until something else stops referencing them.
    <a href="/wiki/commands/prune"><code>puv prune --global</code></a> drops cache entries nothing
    references. A project tool installed with <a href="/wiki/commands/load"><code>load</code></a>
    is a different environment. <code>remove</code> edits the app manifest.
  </p>
</Wiki>
